/*
 * hello.qcl - QCL cli: Hello, World!
 *
 * WHAT: prints the text Hello, World!, using only the classical part of QCL.
 * WHY:  QCL (Quantum Computation Language) has a classical control language
 *       with procedures, and quantum operations are added to it. This file uses
 *       only the classical part, so the program needs no qubits to print its line.
 *       The syntax of procedure and print is the one in the examples that ship
 *       with QCL (lib/deutsch.qcl in the source archive).
 *
 * Source: Bernhard Oemer, the QCL source archive (README: "QCL (Quantum Computation
 * Language) ... a syntax derived from classical procedural languages like C or
 * Pascal"; qcl.cc: "(c) Copyright by Bernhard Oemer, 1998"). See README.md.
 *
 * Output note: each print statement in QCL writes ": " before its text and a
 * space after it, so the line printed is ": Hello, World! ". See README.md.
 */

// A procedure is a named block of classical statements. Its body is in braces.
procedure hello() {
    print "Hello, World!";
}

// Calling the procedure runs it. The statement ends with a semicolon.
hello();
